**Fallback JS**

Use this only if you need to support browsers without scroll-driven animations:
```js
if (!CSS.supports('animation-timeline', 'scroll()')) {
  const bar = document.querySelector('#progress');
  addEventListener('scroll', () => {
    const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - innerHeight;
    bar.style.transform = `scaleX(${max > 0 ? scrollY / max : 0})`;
  }, { passive: true });
}
```
The bar needs `transform-origin: 0 50%` and `transform: scaleX(0)` in base CSS for this path. Also, as written, the fallback runs for reduced-motion users in browsers without support. You may want to gate it on `matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)')`.

**Notes**
- **Compositor-friendly:** it animates `transform: scaleX()` rather than `width`, so it stays on the compositor thread.
- **Accessibility:** an empty, CSS-only element needs no ARIA. If you put text or an icon in it, add `aria-hidden="true"`.
- **Polyfill:** don't use `scroll-timeline-polyfill`. The guide says it's incomplete and has known issues.
- **Decorative bar:** if you treat the bar as purely decorative, you can skip the JS fallback and let it degrade to nothing.
- **Article-only progress:** `scroll(root)` tracks the whole page. If the article is only part of the page, use a view timeline on the article instead. Put `view-timeline: --article block;` on `<article>` and `timeline-scope: --article` on a shared ancestor. Then set `animation-timeline: --article` and `animation-range: contain` on the bar. The bar would then fill as the article crosses the viewport. I haven't tested that variant.

### Modern Web Guidance
- **`scroll-progress-indicator`**: I used `@supports (animation-timeline: scroll())` and put `animation-timeline` after the `animation` shorthand, with the scroll listener only as a fallback. ("declare `animation-timeline` *after* it to prevent the shorthand from resetting the timeline.")

Use a CSS scroll-driven animation. Since 2026-10-27 it's Baseline "Newly available": Chrome/Edge 115, Safari 26, and Firefox 159. Use the native CSS wherever it's supported, and add a small JS fallback for older browsers.

```html
<div id="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>
```

```css
#progress {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0 0 auto 0;          /* top, full width */
  height: 4px;
  background: red;
  transform-origin: 0 50%;
  transform: scaleX(0);       /* start empty; the fallback JS drives this */
  z-index: 10;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    @keyframes grow-progress {
      from { transform: scaleX(0); }
      to   { transform: scaleX(1); }
    }
    #progress {
      animation: grow-progress auto linear;
      animation-timeline: scroll();   /* must come AFTER the shorthand */
    }
  }
}
```

```js
// Fallback for browsers without scroll-driven animations
if (!CSS.supports('animation-timeline', 'scroll()')) {
  const bar = document.querySelector('#progress');
  const update = () => {
    const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - innerHeight;
    bar.style.transform = `scaleX(${max > 0 ? scrollY / max : 0})`;
  };
  addEventListener('scroll', update, { passive: true });
  update();
}
```

- **`scroll()`:** it tracks the nearest scrolling ancestor. A `fixed` element placed in `<body>` therefore tracks the page scroll. Use `scroll(root)` to be explicit.
- **`transform: scaleX`:** it runs on the compositor. Animating `width` would not.
- **Article-only progress:** to track the article rather than the whole page, put the article in its own scroller. You can also use a named `view-timeline` on the `<article>` with `timeline-scope`.
- **`aria-hidden` and fallback:** the bar is an empty div, so `aria-hidden` is harmless. The bundled guide treats purely decorative indicators as progressive enhancement and says to skip the fallback. I kept the fallback because a reading-progress bar is arguably functional.
- **Reduced motion:** in the sketch above, users who prefer reduced motion get an empty bar on supporting browsers. If you still want it for them, drop the `prefers-reduced-motion` wrapper. A progress bar that tracks scroll position isn't really motion.
- **Polyfill:** don't use `scroll-timeline-polyfill`. The guide calls it incomplete and buggy.

